<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  PLAYER play glyph - the owner-supplied FINAL vector.

  Source: tools/figma-export/player-icons/owner-final/play_light.svg and
  play_dark.svg, which carry the identical path and differ only in its stroke.
  These supersede node 2484:59 from ../exact/, which PR #42 installed.

  It is a STROKE, not a filled outline. The superseded glyph was two contours
  wound against each other so a non-zero fill left the middle open; this is one
  open shape with a 4-wide round-capped, round-joined stroke. Different
  construction, so the file is replaced rather than edited.

  **The viewport is the 80x80 control box, and the pathData is verbatim.** The
  supplied file states where the glyph sits inside the surface, and it is not the
  middle - the path spans x 28..51.33, whose centre is 39.665, a third of a unit
  left of 40. Cropping the viewport to the glyph would mean either rewriting the
  coordinates or letting scaleType=center put it somewhere the file does not. So
  the drawable takes the control's own box: every digit below is the supplied
  digit, and the glyph lands exactly where the SVG puts it.

  That makes the drawable 80x80, which is the surface's size - but the surface is
  NOT here. It is bg_player_play_pause, the button's own background, and it stays
  a separate drawable so that PlayerControl can swap this glyph for the buffering
  indicator without the fill ever leaving the screen. What this file holds is one
  path and no rectangle.

  Painted, the stroke reaches 2 beyond the path on every side, so the ink is
  27.33 x 33.9957, which PlayerControlRenderTest measures off the rasterised
  control. That is a property of the path rather than a number set anywhere, so
  it has no dimen of its own.

  The stroke is the repo's #FFFFFF placeholder; the colour is
  `player_play_glyph`, applied from the layout.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="@dimen/player_play_size"
    android:height="@dimen/player_play_size"
    android:viewportWidth="80"
    android:viewportHeight="80">
    <path
        android:fillColor="@android:color/transparent"
        android:pathData="M28 25.0022L51.33 40.0001L28 54.9979V25.0022Z"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFF"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round"
        android:strokeWidth="4" />
</vector>
